/E (Financial and Investment Barriers: While the $400 million investment in January 2025 reflects progress, nuclear projects require significantly higher capital expenditures, often running into billions of dollars. The high cost of SMRs, despite their scalability, may deter widespread adoption without sufficient financial backing.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
271 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 292 0 R
/E (Regulatory and Policy Hurdles: The nuclear industry operates under stringent international regulations to ensure safety and security. Many African nations lack the robust regulatory frameworks needed to oversee nuclear development effectively. Establishing independent regulatory bodies, aligning with International Atomic Energy Agency \(IAEA\) standards, and ensuring public trust in nuclear governance will be crucial.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
272 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 292 0 R
/E (Technological and Infrastructure Constraints: Deploying nuclear energy, especially SMRs, requires specialised infrastructure, including reactors, cooling systems, and waste management facilities. Many African countries lack the necessary infrastructure and supply chain networks, leading to dependency on foreign expertise and materials. This reliance may increase costs and delay project timelines.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
